---
import QuestionLayout from '@layouts/QuestionLayout.astro';

const relatedLinks = [];
const _body = "<p>The browser adapter is a Traverse placement target that runs WASM capabilities directly inside the user's browser. It uses the browser's native WebAssembly API and applies the same contract validation as every other Traverse target. The capability behaves identically whether it is called from the browser or from a server.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why run capabilities in the browser</h2>\n      <p>The main reason is consistency. If your pricing logic runs as a WASM capability on your API, you can load the same binary in the browser for instant previews. The user sees the correct number before they submit a form. No extra round-trip. No risk of the frontend computing a different result than the backend.</p>\n\n      <p>This is the core promise of Traverse placement targets. The contract is the invariant. The adapter is just where the execution happens.</p>\n\n      <h2>How it works</h2>\n      <p>The public Web/TypeScript embedder SDK (<code>traverse-embedder-web</code>) is what runs inside the browser. Its <code>BundleEmbedder</code> compiles each bundled capability module with <code>WebAssembly.compile</code>, validates its imports against the same host-ABI allowlist the native executor enforces — no filesystem, no network, no environment access — and instantiates and invokes it synchronously through a minimal WASI shim that pipes JSON in and out. There is no separate crate that \"runs inside\" the browser to make this happen; the browser is already a WebAssembly host, so the SDK talks to it directly.</p>\n\n      <ul>\n        <li>Preconditions are validated before execution, same as on the server</li>\n        <li>Postconditions are validated after the WASM function returns</li>\n        <li>The result object includes a trace artifact for debugging</li>\n        <li>Errors are typed and match the contract's error schema</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Current status</h2>\n      <p>Native is still the most mature target, but the Web embedder SDK is a shipped path: it executes governed bundles directly in the browser's native WebAssembly host, with digest verification on every capability. It's scoped to linear, directly-triggered pipelines today — conditional and event-driven workflow edges are rejected at initialization rather than silently mis-executed. See <a href=\"/platforms.html\">Platforms</a> for the full, evidence-linked breakdown of what's shipped versus in progress.</p>";
const _jsonLd = "{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"FAQPage\",\"mainEntity\":[{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"What is the browser adapter?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"The browser adapter is a Traverse placement target that runs WASM capabilities inside the browser using the WebAssembly API. It applies the same contract validation as every other target, so capabilities execute identically whether the call comes from a browser, edge worker, or server.\"}}]}";
---
<QuestionLayout
  title={"What is the browser adapter?"}
  description={"The Traverse browser adapter lets you run contract-governed WASM capabilities directly in the browser. Same contracts, same behavior, zero server round-trip."}
  canonical={"https://traverse-framework.com/questions/what-is-the-browser-adapter.html"}
  category={"Getting Started"}
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
  jsonLd={_jsonLd}
>
  <Fragment set:html={_body} />
</QuestionLayout>
